  "final_span": ". He (Forbes) was seated alongside several other patients, and at the time when my administration had a roster, they assigned me to duty on Tuesday/Wednesday nights for overnight shifts, which meant I was present and fulfilled my part in the triage. It was late into the night when we went through 30 patients, and we concluded that only three were true emergency cases based on the triage policy.\n\"So we call about one bundle of emergency patients first according to triage and the rest are second bucket, which is the urgent bucket, and then you have... the less urgent bucket. So with that large number of patients, we had to triage the system very diligently,\" Dr Dawes said.\nOn an average day, emergency treatment accounts for up to six hours of work time for the general surgeon, who tends to see 30 to 40 patients during their shift. Patients categorized as \"non-urgent\" spend their wait time in the common area, an area where typically three to four wheelchairs are present.\n\"We are not above criticism but if you are going to look at this entire thing you must look at the infrastructure, you must look at the social it transformed in Jamaica, you must look at the number of these people coming in with non-emergency BP and other ailments, and then you must look at the lack of adequate staffing, lack of flow in a very cramped place, and then you must look at is the patients not acting responsibly, going to the hospital in the night because they can go there for free rather than going to a health centre,\" Dr Dawes stressed.\nHe argued that seeing patients early at a health centre could reduce night-time visits to emergency by up to 70 per cent. Dr Dawes noted that the doctors looked into redirecting visitors from health centers to emergency centers but ultimately did not address the issue further.\n\"If somebody doesn't act like an adult, we aren't going to support them. I've told you my account of what, I think, should've happened and something we are getting used to, but we're not excusing ourselves if that account doesn't match up to what actually happened",
